{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FYYZhUohKQuQq8ii7MiPLU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map — event-gated-adaptive-horizon\\\\n# Last updated: 2026-05-15\\\\n# Total: 76 papers (72 existing + 4 incremental scan 2026-05-15)\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# EXISTING CITATIONS (already in paper.tex)\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: sutton1999options\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Between MDPs and Semi-MDPs: A Framework for Temporal Abstraction in Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sutton R.S., Precup D., Singh S.]\\\\n  year: 1999\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"Artificial Intelligence 112(1-2):181-211\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Foundational options framework for temporal abstraction in RL; our work investigates why temporal abstraction fails in world-model RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hafner2020dreamerv1\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Dream to Control: Learning Behaviors by Latent Imagination\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hafner D., Lillicrap T., Ba J., Norouzi M.]\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"DreamerV1 — base architecture family; our experiments build on the RSSM and imagination-based training paradigm\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hafner2023dreamerv3\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Mastering Diverse Domains through World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hafner D., Pasukonis J., Ba J., Lillicrap T.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2301.04104 (Nature 2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"DreamerV3 — the specific base system we modify; symlog transforms, discrete latents, all default hyperparameters come from here\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: sharma2017figar\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Fine Grained Action Repetition for Deep Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sharma S., Lakshminarayanan A.S., Ravindran B.]\\\\n  year: 2017\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR Workshop\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"FiGAR — learns action-repeat durations in model-free RL; we extend the idea to imagination step size in world-model RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: janner2019mbpo\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"When to Trust Your Model: Model-Based Policy Optimization\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Janner M., Fu J., Zhang M., Levine S.]\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"MBPO — formalizes model exploitation in MBRL; our conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) is a specific instance of model exploitation tied to temporal embedding\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Multi-step prediction error compounding\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: ke2019learning\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Learning Dynamics Model in Reinforcement Learning by Incorporating the Long Term Future\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Ke N.R., Singh A., Touati A., Goyal A., Bengio Y., Parikh D., Batra D.]\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Addresses long-term dynamics prediction via latent-variable autoregressive model; directly relevant to our finding that multi-step RSSM predictions degrade with dt>1\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: asadi2019combating\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Combating the Compounding-Error Problem with a Multi-step Model\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Asadi K., Misra D., Kim S., Littman M.L.]\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:1905.13320\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Proposes multi-step models to bypass compounding error; our work shows that even with multi-step imagination, the dt-embedding creates a different failure mode (value overestimation) distinct from compounding error\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: venkatraman2015improving\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Improving Multi-Step Prediction of Learned Time Series Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Venkatraman A., Hebert M., Bagnell J.A.]\\\\n  year: 2015\\\\n  venue: AAAI\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Data-as-Demonstrator (DaD) for robust multi-step prediction; establishes theoretical guarantee relating training error to multi-step prediction error\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lambert2022investigating\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Investigating Compounding Prediction Errors in Learned Dynamics Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lambert N., Pister K.S.J., Calandra R.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2203.09637\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Empirical study of compounding error properties; we rule out compounding error as the primary failure mechanism (008c vs 008a comparison) and identify value overestimation instead\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Model-predictive control with learned dynamics\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: chua2018pets\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Deep Reinforcement Learning in a Handful of Trials using Probabilistic Dynamics Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Chua K., Calandra R., McAllister R., Levine S.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"PETS — ensemble-based uncertainty-aware MPC; uses explicit uncertainty quantification to avoid model exploitation, contrasting with DreamerV3's implicit approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nagabandi2018neural\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Neural Network Dynamics for Model-Based Deep Reinforcement Learning with Model-Free Fine-Tuning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nagabandi A., Kahn G., Fearing R.S., Levine S.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: ICRA\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Demonstrates NN dynamics + MPC for locomotion; model errors accumulate over prediction horizon, motivating our investigation of horizon-dependent failures\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hansen2024tdmpc2\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"TD-MPC2: Scalable, Robust World Models for Continuous Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hansen N., Su H., Wang X.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR (arXiv:2310.16828)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"TD-MPC2 — SOTA MPC planning route (online planning + learned dynamics); DMC benchmark leader but orthogonal to imagination-based training — reviewer defense: TD-MPC2 does online MPC rollouts at test time, we study actor-critic training via imagined rollouts (DreamerV3 paradigm), not a same-level comparison\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Pessimistic MBRL / model exploitation mitigation\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: yu2020mopo\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"MOPO: Model-based Offline Policy Optimization\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Yu T., Thomas G., Yu L., Ermon S., Zou J., Levine S., Finn C., Ma T.]\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS (arXiv:2005.13239)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"MOPO — pessimistic MBRL via uncertainty-penalized reward (reward -= λ * model_uncertainty); generic model exploitation mitigation — reviewer defense: MOPO addresses distribution shift in offline MBRL, our dt=1 imagination restriction targets dt_embedding-induced CIVO (temporal conditioning corruption), two complementary mechanisms\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Value overestimation mitigation\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: thrun1993issues\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Issues in Using Function Approximation for Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Thrun S., Schwartz A.]\\\\n  year: 1993\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"Connectionist Models Summer School\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"First identification of systematic value overestimation from function approximation; our CIVO extends this to model-based imagination with temporal conditioning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: vanhasselt2016double\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Deep Reinforcement Learning with Double Q-Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [van Hasselt H., Guez A., Silver D.]\\\\n  year: 2016\\\\n  venue: AAAI\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Double DQN — mitigates overestimation via double estimator; our finding that trained dt-embedding enables value exploitation is a complementary overestimation mechanism in MBRL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: fujimoto2018td3\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Addressing Function Approximation Error in Actor-Critic Methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Fujimoto S., van Hoof H., Meger D.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"TD3 — clipped double-Q to limit overestimation in actor-critic; DreamerV3 uses a single critic, making it more vulnerable to the overestimation we identify\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: haarnoja2018sac\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Soft Actor-Critic: Off-Policy Maximum Entropy Deep Reinforcement Learning with a Stochastic Actor\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Haarnoja T., Zhou A., Abbeel P., Levine S.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"SAC — entropy regularization implicitly mitigates overestimation; relevant contrast to DreamerV3's actor which can greedily exploit overestimated values\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lee2021sunrise\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SUNRISE: A Simple Unified Framework for Ensemble Learning in Deep Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lee K., Laskin M., Srinivas A., Abbeel P.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Ensemble-based uncertainty weighting for value estimation; potential mitigation for our identified CIVO mechanism\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Delusional bias\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lu2018nondelusional\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-Iteration\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lu T., Schuurmans D., Boutilier C.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Formalizes delusional bias from policy-class constraints in Q-learning; we identify an analogous phenomenon where temporal conditioning enables the critic to exploit model imprecision\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Hierarchical RL with learned models\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hafner2022director\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Deep Hierarchical Planning from Pixels\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hafner D., Lee K.-H., Fischer I., Abbeel P.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Director — hierarchical planning in Dreamer latent space via subgoals; addresses temporal abstraction through goal hierarchy rather than variable dt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nachum2018hiro\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Data-Efficient Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nachum O., Gu S., Lee H., Levine S.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"HIRO — off-policy HRL with learned subgoals; demonstrates that hierarchical temporal abstraction can improve sample efficiency when properly designed\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Action repeat / frame skip\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: braylan2015frame\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Frame Skip Is a Powerful Parameter for Learning to Play Atari\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Braylan A., Hollenbeck M., Meyerson E., Miikkulainen R.]\\\\n  year: 2015\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"AAAI Workshop on Learning for General Competency in Video Games\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Empirical study showing frame skip strongly affects Atari performance; motivates investigation of temporal granularity as a critical design choice\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lakshminarayanan2017dynamic\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Dynamic Action Repetition for Deep Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lakshminarayanan A.S., Sharma S., Ravindran B.]\\\\n  year: 2017\\\\n  venue: AAAI\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Learns per-step action repetition duration; model-free analog of our variable-dt imagination approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: biedenkapp2021temporl\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"TempoRL: Learning When to Act\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Biedenkapp A., Rajan R., Hutter F., Lindauer M.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Learns action persistence via skip-policy; demonstrates up to 10x speedup — but in model-free setting without the model exploitation risk we identify\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: DreamerV2\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hafner2021dreamerv2\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Mastering Atari with Discrete World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hafner D., Lillicrap T., Norouzi M., Ba J.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"DreamerV2 — introduces discrete latent representations used in DreamerV3; key architectural predecessor to our base system\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Alternative world model architectures\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: micheli2023iris\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Transformers are Sample-Efficient World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Micheli V., Alonso E., Fleuret F.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"IRIS — autoregressive transformer world model; alternative to RSSM that may exhibit different compounding error characteristics under temporal abstraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: robine2023twm\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Transformer-based World Models Are Happy With 100k Interactions\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Robine J., Hoeftmann M., Uelwer T., Harmeling S.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"TWM — Transformer-XL world model with direct state access; circumvents RSSM's recurrent compression that exacerbates our identified prediction gap\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: chen2022transdreamer\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"TransDreamer: Reinforcement Learning with Transformer World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Chen C., Wu Y.-F., Yoon J., Ahn S.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2202.09481\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Replaces RSSM with Transformer State-Space Model; directly tests whether RSSM-specific architecture contributes to temporal abstraction failures\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: 2024-2026 concurrent work on temporal abstraction in world models\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: gumbsch2024thick\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Learning Hierarchical World Models with Adaptive Temporal Abstractions from Discrete Latent Dynamics\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Gumbsch C., Sajid N., Martius G., Butz M.V.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"THICK — learns adaptive temporal abstractions via context-change detection; closest concurrent work — addresses temporal abstraction in Dreamer but via hierarchy rather than variable-dt imagination\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: mattes2024hieros\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Hieros: Hierarchical Imagination on Structured State Space Sequence World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Mattes P., Schlosser R., Herbrich R.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Multi-scale hierarchical imagination with S5 world model; demonstrates temporal abstraction via hierarchy avoids the single-scale dt-embedding failure we identify\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Seed-dependent instability & value overestimation in MBRL\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: henderson2018deep\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Henderson P., Islam R., Bachman P., Pineau J., Precup D., Meger D.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: AAAI\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Foundational study on seed variance in deep RL — shows statistically different performance distributions arise from random seeds alone with identical hyperparameters; directly supports our observation that seed 0 vs seed 2 produce qualitatively different value dynamics under the same dt_embed_dim=0 config\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nikishin2022primacy\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The Primacy Bias in Deep Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nikishin E., Schwarzer M., D'Oro P., Bacon P.-L., Courville A.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Identifies primacy bias — agents overfit early data and lose ability to learn from new transitions, leading to delayed performance collapse; parallels our finding where value overestimation initially appears benign but becomes pathological later, and the collapse timing is seed-dependent\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: qiao2023mind\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Mind the Model, Not the Agent: The Primacy Bias in Model-based RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Qiao Z., Lyu J., Li X.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2310.15017 (ECAI 2024)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Extends primacy bias to MBRL, testing on DreamerV2 — shows world model primacy bias dominates over agent primacy bias; directly relevant to Dreamer-family training instability and explains why some seeds recover (model escapes early overfitting) while others don't\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hussing2024dissecting\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Dissecting Deep RL with High Update Ratios: Combatting Value Divergence\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hussing M., Voelcker C.A., Gilitschenski I., Farahmand A.-M., Eaton E.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning Journal (RLC 2024)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows value overestimation and Q-divergence under high update-to-data ratios, driven by optimizer momentum — overestimation occurs on both in-distribution and OOD data; mechanism maps to our CIVO where imagination-trained critic inflates values progressively, with divergence onset sensitive to initialization\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: barkley2024stealing\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Stealing That Free Lunch: Exposing the Limits of Dyna-Style Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Barkley B., Fridovich-Keil D.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2412.14312 (ICML 2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Demonstrates that synthetic rollouts (imagination) can degrade performance inconsistently across environments, with overestimation bias as a contributing factor; supports our finding that imagination-based training creates environment- and seed-dependent failure modes that existing mitigation techniques do not reliably fix\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: bjorck2022variance\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Is High Variance Unavoidable in RL? A Case Study in Continuous Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Bjorck J., Gomes C.P., Weinberger K.Q.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows RL variance mostly arises early in training from poor 'outlier' runs caused by numerical instability; parallels our observation that seed 2 diverges early while seed 0 recovers — the reward prediction degradation from removing dt_embedding may create exactly this early-training instability\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Reward prediction quality → MBRL performance\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Our key finding: removing dt_embedding causes reward_MAE to spike 75x\\\\n# (0.01 → 0.747), making performance seed-dependent. These papers\\\\n# establish that reward prediction quality is a known MBRL bottleneck.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lee2024dreamsmooth\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DreamSmooth: Improving Model-based Reinforcement Learning via Reward Smoothing\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lee V., Abbeel P., Lee Y.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Directly demonstrates reward prediction is a bottleneck in MBRL — sparse/ambiguous rewards cause the reward model to predict zeros, creating gradient-free landscapes during imagination; our dt_embed_dim=0 ablation creates an analogous bottleneck where the reward head loses temporal conditioning and MAE spikes 75x\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: ma2024harmonydream\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"HarmonyDream: Task Harmonization Inside World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Ma H., Wu J., Feng N., Xiao C., Li D., Hao J., Wang J., Long M.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: ICML\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows observation prediction dominates reward prediction in world model training, degrading reward model quality; proposes dynamic loss balancing for 10-69% gains — directly relevant to our finding that dt_embedding removal disrupts the observation-reward prediction balance and causes reward MAE explosion\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lambert2020objective\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Objective Mismatch in Model-based Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lambert N., Amos B., Yadan O., Calandra R.]\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"L4DC (Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, vol. 120)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Formalizes that one-step prediction likelihood does not correlate with control performance — the 'objective mismatch' problem; our work reveals a specific instance: the reward head's prediction accuracy (measured by MAE) directly gates whether the critic can learn stable value estimates, creating a reward-specific objective mismatch\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Auxiliary tasks & representation coupling in world models\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Our finding: reward prediction quality is tightly coupled to temporal\\\\n# conditioning (dt_embedding). Removing the embedding degrades the\\\\n# reward head while dynamics remain intact — suggesting the reward head\\\\n# acts as more than a passive predictor; it conditions the shared\\\\n# representation. Literature gap: no prior work studies this coupling\\\\n# specifically in Dreamer-family world models.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: jaderberg2017unreal\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning with Unsupervised Auxiliary Tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Jaderberg M., Mnih V., Czarnecki W.M., Schaul T., Leibo J.Z., Silver D., Kavukcuoglu K.]\\\\n  year: 2017\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"UNREAL — introduces reward prediction as an auxiliary task that shapes shared representations; foundational evidence that reward prediction is not just a passive output but actively conditions what features the network learns — our finding that removing dt_embedding selectively degrades reward prediction (but not dynamics) suggests the temporal conditioning specifically enables reward-relevant feature extraction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lyle2021auxiliary\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"On The Effect of Auxiliary Tasks on Representation Dynamics\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lyle C., Rowland M., Ostrovski G., Dabney W.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: AISTATS\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Theoretical analysis showing auxiliary tasks shape representations toward top eigenvectors of the transition operator; provides formal grounding for why removing temporal conditioning from the reward head could alter the entire representation — the reward head's gradients contribute to spectral structure of the shared latent space\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: DreamerV3 architecture improvements (2025-2026)\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: bredis2026nedreamer\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Next Embedding Prediction Makes World Models Stronger\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Bredis G., Balagansky N., Gavrilov D., Rakhimov R.]\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2603.02765\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"NE-Dreamer — decoder-free MBRL replacing pixel reconstruction with next-embedding prediction via temporal transformer + Barlow Twins; improves RSSM prediction quality on DMC and DMLab but does not address temporal abstraction or CIVO — orthogonal to our dt-embedding diagnosis\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: zhang2025dymodreamer\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DyMoDreamer: World Modeling with Dynamic Modulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Zhang B., Wang R., Xiao W., Zhang W., Sun J., Huang G., Chen J., Wang G.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Modulates RSSM via inter-frame differencing mask and stochastic categorical dynamic modulation; SOTA on Atari 100k (156.6% HNS) and DMC (832) — improves dynamics extraction but does not address variable-rate imagination or dt_embedding-induced value overestimation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Temporal abstraction methods\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: liu2025vanta\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Semantic Temporal Abstraction via Vision-Language Model Guidance for Efficient Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Liu T.-S., Liu X.-H., Chen R., Jin L., Wang P., Zhang Z., Yu Y.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: ICLR\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"VanTA — VLM-guided trajectory decomposition into semantically meaningful skills via vector quantization + relabeling; operates at high-level skill abstraction (Franka Kitchen, Crafter) — contrasts with our low-level dt-based temporal abstraction within world-model imagination steps\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW: Evaluation methodology\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: agarwal2021precipice\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Deep Reinforcement Learning at the Edge of the Statistical Precipice\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Agarwal R., Schwarzer M., Castro P.S., Courville A.C., Bellemare M.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Proposes rliable — interval estimates, performance profiles, IQM for robust multi-seed RL evaluation (NeurIPS Outstanding Paper); our multi-seed experiment design follows this methodology to ensure statistical validity of collapse detection across seeds\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14 gap analysis): Time discretization theory\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Gap: variable timestep / time discretization invariance — foundational\\\\n# theory for why dt matters and how to handle it.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: tallec2019making\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Making Deep Q-learning methods Robust to Time Discretization\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Tallec C., Blier L., Ollivier Y.]\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICML (arXiv:1901.09732)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows Q-learning performance is highly sensitive to time discretization δ; proposes architectural + exploration modifications for δ-invariance. Theoretical foundation for why our variable-dt imagination introduces a fundamental difficulty: RSSM trained at δ=1 cannot produce δ-invariant predictions at dt>1\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: park2021sar\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Time Discretization-Invariant Safe Action Repetition for Policy Gradient Methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Park S., Kim J., Kim G.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS (arXiv:2111.03941)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"SAR — δ-invariant action repetition for policy gradients; achieves time-discretization robustness in model-free RL via adaptive reaction to state changes during repetition. Contrasts with our finding that world-model RL lacks this robustness when imagination uses dt>1\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: wang2024seac\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning with Elastic Time Steps\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Wang D., Beltrame G.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2402.14961\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"SEAC/MOSEAC — variable-duration time steps where agent selects both action and duration; model-free approach that explicitly treats action duration as part of the action space. Our work reveals why this strategy is problematic in world-model RL: the critic cannot reliably evaluate variable-duration imagined trajectories\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14 gap analysis): Delusional bias mitigation\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: su2020conqur\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"ConQUR: Mitigating Delusional Bias in Deep Q-learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Su D., Ooi J., Lu T., Schuurmans D., Boutilier C.]\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICML (arXiv:2002.12399)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Follow-up to Lu et al. 2018; mitigates delusional bias via consistency-penalized Q-labels. Addresses delusional bias from policy-class constraints in Q-learning; our CIVO is an analogous phenomenon in MBRL where temporal conditioning (dt_embedding) creates inconsistent value targets across imagination steps\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14 gap analysis): Diagnostic methodology in RL\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Gap: our paper is a diagnostic analysis — need methodological\\\\n# precedents for \\\\\\\"what matters\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"implementation matters\\\\\\\" style papers.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: engstrom2020implementation\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Implementation Matters in Deep Policy Gradients: A Case Study on PPO and TRPO\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Engstrom L., Ilyas A., Santurkar S., Tsipras D., Janoos F., Rudolph L., Madry A.]\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR (arXiv:2005.12729)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Demonstrates that code-level implementation details (not algorithmic innovations) drive most of PPO's gains over TRPO; methodological precedent for our diagnostic approach — we similarly show that a specific implementation choice (trained vs. untrained dt_embedding) is the primary factor determining collapse vs. stability\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: andrychowicz2021whatmatters\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"What Matters for On-Policy Deep Actor-Critic Methods? A Large-Scale Study\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Andrychowicz M., Raichuk A., Stańczyk P., Orsini M., Girgin S., Marinier R., Hussenot L., Geist M., Pietquin O., Michalski M., Gelly S., Bachem O.]\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR (arXiv:2006.05990)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Large-scale factorial study of 50+ design choices in on-policy RL (250K+ agents); methodological template for our factorial experiment design isolating dt_embedding × subsample × horizon effects. Demonstrates that systematic ablation reveals non-obvious interactions — parallel to our finding that dt_embedding training status interacts with subsample and horizon\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14 gap analysis): MBRL rollout quality & value overestimation\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Gap: recent ICLR 2025 papers directly studying imagination rollout\\\\n# quality and value overestimation in model-based settings.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: frauenknecht2025rollouts\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"On Rollouts in Model-Based Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Frauenknecht B., Subhasish D., Solowjow F., Trimpe S.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR (arXiv:2501.16918)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows trajectory-sampling rollouts diverge from ground truth after few steps and can harm policy learning; proposes Infoprop separating aleatoric/epistemic uncertainty with rollout termination criteria. Directly supports our finding that dt>1 imagination produces corrupted data — our work identifies a specific mechanism (dt_embedding-induced value exploitation) beyond general compounding error\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: voelcker2025madtd\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"MAD-TD: Model-Augmented Data stabilizes High Update Ratio RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Voelcker C., Hussing M., Eaton E., Farahmand A.-M., Gilitschenski I.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR (arXiv:2410.08896)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows value function misgeneralization to on-policy actions causes instability at high UTD ratios; uses model-generated data to improve value accuracy. Complementary to our finding: MAD-TD addresses value misgeneralization from replay distribution mismatch, we address value overestimation from temporal conditioning corruption in imagination\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14 gap analysis): Latest action repeat methods\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nie2025sdar\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Select before Act: Spatially Decoupled Action Repetition for Continuous Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nie B., et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR (arXiv:2502.06919)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"SDAR — per-dimension action repetition decisions for flexible temporal persistence in model-free RL; SOTA on DMC continuous control. Advances action repeat in model-free setting; our work shows the fundamental obstacle to analogous variable-rate approaches in model-based RL (CIVO from dt_embedding)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14 gap analysis): DreamerV4 / latest Dreamer evolution\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: hafner2025dreamerv4\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Training Agents Inside of Scalable World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hafner D., Yan W., Lillicrap T.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2509.24527\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"DreamerV4 — replaces RSSM with efficient transformer world model + shortcut forcing; scales to video pretraining (Minecraft diamonds from video). Architectural successor to DreamerV3; our dt_embedding diagnosis is specific to RSSM but the broader lesson (temporal conditioning must match model fidelity) applies to transformer world models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14): Training instability dynamics\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: zhang2026instability\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Training instability in deep learning follows low-dimensional dynamical principles\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Zhang L., et al.]\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026 (arXiv:2601.13160)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Training instability follows low-dimensional dynamical principles; provides theoretical framework for why seed-dependent collapse vs self-correction occurs in our experiments (008d seed 1 collapses, 009b seed 2 self-corrects under identical config)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# NEW (2026-05-14): Seed-Dependent Stochastic Outcomes in MBRL\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Dedicated literature survey for the stochastic collapse phenomenon:\\\\n# same config (trained dt_embedding + subsample + H=8) produces\\\\n# collapse in seed 0 (008d) but self-correction in seed 2 (009b).\\\\n# nve>1.5x is a 100% reliable marker of trained dt_embedding,\\\\n# but collapse itself is stochastic (1/2 seeds).\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: sokar2023dormant\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The Dormant Neuron Phenomenon in Deep Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sokar G., Agarwal R., Castro P.S., Evci U.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Identifies dormant neurons — inactive units accumulating during RL training that reduce network expressivity. Seed-dependent because different random initializations produce different early dormancy patterns; directly explains why some seeds lose the representational capacity needed to self-correct value overestimation (our seed 0 collapses) while others retain enough active neurons to recover (our seed 2 self-corrects)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lyle2022capacity\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Understanding and Preventing Capacity Loss in Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lyle C., Rowland M., Dabney W.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2023 (arXiv:2204.09560)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows feature rank collapse from non-stationary TD targets is a necessary condition for learning failure but not sufficient — sparse rewards and other factors must co-occur. Directly parallels our finding: nve>1.5x (value overestimation from trained dt_embedding) is a necessary precondition for collapse but not a sufficient one; additional stochastic factors (initialization, early replay composition) determine whether collapse actually occurs\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: kumar2022dr3\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DR3: Value-Based Deep Reinforcement Learning Requires Explicit Regularization\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Kumar A., Agarwal R., Ma T., Courville A., Tucker G., Levine S.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2022\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows implicit regularization from TD learning favors degenerate feature aliasing (states on both sides of Bellman backup become indistinguishable), creating the substrate for value overestimation. In our setting, trained dt_embedding amplifies this aliasing across different temporal horizons — the critic cannot distinguish between dt=1 and dt>1 value targets, enabling exploitation. Feature aliasing is seed-dependent because it depends on initialization geometry\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: colas2018seeds\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"How Many Random Seeds? Statistical Power Analysis in Deep Reinforcement Learning Experiments\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Colas C., Sigaud O., Oudeyer P.-Y.]\\\\n  year: 2018\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:1806.08295\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Provides statistical framework for determining seed count needed for significant algorithm comparison; our 2-seed observation (1/2 collapse) has limited statistical power by their analysis — but the qualitative difference (nve 4.12x vs 0.38x) is large enough that even 2 seeds establish the stochastic nature of the phenomenon. Methodological basis for future multi-seed validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nikishin2023plasticity\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Deep Reinforcement Learning with Plasticity Injection\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nikishin E., Oh J., Ostrovski G., Lyle C., Pascanu R., Dabney W., Barreto A.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Plasticity injection augments network plasticity without changing parameter count; serves as a diagnostic tool — if injection improves performance, plasticity loss was the bottleneck. Directly applicable to diagnosing whether our seed-dependent collapse is plasticity-mediated: if plasticity injection prevents collapse in seed 0 but has no effect on seed 2 (which already self-corrects), it confirms plasticity loss as the bifurcation mechanism\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: schwarzer2023bbf\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Bigger, Better, Faster: Human-level Atari with Human-level Efficiency\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Schwarzer M., Obando-Ceron J.S., Courville A., Bellemare M., Agarwal R., Castro P.S.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"BBF achieves SOTA sample efficiency via periodic network resets + scaling + weight decay; periodic resets prevent primacy bias accumulation across seeds. Suggests our CIVO collapse might be preventable with periodic critic resets — the reset would break the positive feedback loop between overestimated values and actor exploitation before it becomes irreversible\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: farebrother2024stopregressing\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Stop Regressing: Training Value Functions via Classification for Scalable Deep RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Farebrother J., Orbay J., Vuong Q., Taiga A.A., Chebotar Y., Xiao T., Irpan A., Levine S., Castro P.S., Faust A., Kumar A., Agarwal R.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows categorical cross-entropy for value training provides implicit regularization against overestimation vs MSE regression. DreamerV3 already uses categorical value prediction (twohot + symlog) — yet our trained dt_embedding still induces collapse in some seeds, demonstrating that categorical value training is insufficient to prevent dt_embedding-induced value exploitation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lyle2024normalization\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Normalization and Effective Learning Rates in Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lyle C., Zheng Z., Giunchiglia E., Sherstan C., Hashemizadeh M., Schuurmans D., Dabney W.]\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows normalization layers combat overestimation but cause effective learning rate decay via parameter norm growth. Provides a potential mechanism for seed 2's self-correction: if parameter norms grow faster in certain initializations, effective LR decays faster, naturally damping value overestimation before it cascades into collapse. Seed 0's slower norm growth → sustained high effective LR → overestimation compounds → collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n  survey_tag: seed-dependent-stochastic-outcomes\\\\n\\\\n# --- Survey synthesis: Seed-Dependent Stochastic Outcomes ---\\\\n# Summary (2026-05-14):\\\\n#\\\\n# 1. STOCHASTIC COLLAPSE HAS PRECEDENT:\\\\n#    Plasticity loss (Lyle 2022, Sokar 2023, Nikishin 2023) is inherently\\\\n#    seed-dependent because it depends on initialization → early gradient\\\\n#    dynamics → neuron activity patterns. Different seeds hit different\\\\n#    plasticity trajectories under identical configs.\\\\n#\\\\n# 2. nve>1.5x AS DIAGNOSTIC MARKER (necessary but not sufficient):\\\\n#    Lyle 2022 shows feature rank collapse is necessary but not sufficient\\\\n#    for learning failure. Kumar 2022 shows feature aliasing from TD\\\\n#    learning creates the overestimation substrate. Our nve>1.5x is\\\\n#    analogous: it indicates the dt_embedding has enabled value exploitation\\\\n#    (100% reliable marker), but collapse requires additional conditions\\\\n#    (plasticity loss, insufficient effective LR decay) that are stochastic.\\\\n#\\\\n# 3. SELF-CORRECTION MECHANISM:\\\\n#    Lyle 2024 provides a candidate mechanism: normalization-induced\\\\n#    effective LR decay can naturally damp overestimation in some seeds.\\\\n#    Farebrother 2024 shows categorical value training provides partial\\\\n#    implicit regularization. Together these explain why seed 2 self-corrects:\\\\n#    the combination of DreamerV3's categorical critic + normalization +\\\\n#    favorable initialization produces enough implicit regularization to\\\\n#    overcome the dt_embedding-induced overestimation.\\\\n#\\\\n# 4. THEORETICAL GROUNDING:\\\\n#    Zhang 2026 (already in map) provides the dynamical systems framework.\\\\n#    Bjorck 2022 (already in map) shows early outlier events dominate\\\\n#    variance. Together with the new papers, the framing is:\\\\n#    \\\\\\\"trained dt_embedding creates a necessary condition (nve>1.5x) for\\\\n#    CIVO collapse, but collapse is a stochastic bifurcation\\\\n#    whose outcome depends on seed-dependent plasticity dynamics.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# INCREMENTAL SCAN (2026-05-14): Δt conditioning & adaptive horizon\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 8 new papers from targeted search on:\\\\n#   - Δt/timestep conditioning in world models\\\\n#   - Variable-rate imagination / adaptive horizon\\\\n#   - Hierarchical planning with latent world models (2025-2026)\\\\n#\\\\n# KEY FINDING: TAWM (Nhu et al. ICML 2025) explicitly conditions\\\\n# world model on Δt — closest methodological overlap. But TAWM\\\\n# uses TD-MPC2 (online MPC) not imagination-based actor-critic,\\\\n# and treats Δt conditioning as beneficial. Our work diagnoses\\\\n# Δt conditioning as pathological in imagination-based training\\\\n# (CIVO). Complementary, not duplicative.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: nhu2025tawm\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Time-Aware World Model for Adaptive Prediction and Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nhu A.N., Son S., Lin M.C.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025 (arXiv:2506.08441)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"TAWM — explicitly conditions world model on time-step size Δt and trains over diverse Δt values for multi-rate dynamics learning. Built on TD-MPC2 (online MPC). KEY DISTINCTION from our work: (1) TAWM treats Δt conditioning as beneficial for performance/data-efficiency; we diagnose it as pathological in imagination-based actor-critic training (CIVO). (2) TAWM uses MPC (no imagination rollouts for policy training); our failure mode is specific to imagination-based training where the critic exploits dt-conditioned value estimates. (3) TAWM focuses on observation-rate robustness; we focus on value overestimation from temporal conditioning corruption. TAWM actually strengthens our contribution: it shows Δt conditioning works in MPC settings, making our finding that it fails in imagination-based settings a surprising and important diagnostic.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n  novelty_threat: \\\\\\\"MEDIUM — methodological overlap (Δt conditioning) but fundamentally different paradigm (MPC vs imagination) and opposite conclusion (beneficial vs pathological). Must cite and differentiate in paper.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: li2025lsimagine\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Open-World Reinforcement Learning over Long Short-Term Imagination\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Li J., Wang Q., Wang Y., Jin X., Li Y., Zeng W., Yang X.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025 Oral (arXiv:2410.03618)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"LS-Imagine — extends imagination horizon in Dreamer via goal-conditioned jumpy state transitions and affordance maps. Addresses the 'short-sightedness' of standard Dreamer imagination. KEY DISTINCTION: LS-Imagine adds jumpy transitions to reach long-horizon targets (open-world Minecraft), while our work shows that variable-dt imagination itself introduces CIVO. LS-Imagine's jumpy transitions use goal conditioning (not dt-embedding), potentially avoiding the value overestimation we identify.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n  novelty_threat: \\\\\\\"LOW — different mechanism (goal-conditioned jumps vs dt-embedding) and different focus (long-horizon exploration vs diagnostic of failure modes). Should cite as concurrent approach to extending imagination horizon.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: zhang2026hwm\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Hierarchical Planning with Latent World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Zhang W., Terver B., Zholus A., Chitnis S., Sutaria H., Assran M., Balestriero R., Bar A., Bardes A., LeCun Y., Ballas N.]\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2604.03208\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: direct-competitor\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"HWM — learns multi-scale latent world models for hierarchical MPC planning; high-level planner optimizes macro-actions, low-level planner handles primitives. 70% success on real-world non-greedy robotic tasks vs 0% for single-level. Addresses temporal abstraction via hierarchical planning (MPC), not imagination-based actor-critic. Further evidence that temporal abstraction in world models requires hierarchy rather than single-scale variable-dt approaches.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n  novelty_threat: \\\\\\\"LOW — MPC-based hierarchical planning, not imagination-based training. Supports our thesis that flat dt-embedding approaches are inferior to hierarchical alternatives.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: kobayashi2025metacontroller\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Emergent temporal abstractions in autoregressive models enable hierarchical reinforcement learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Kobayashi S., et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2512.20605\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"MetaController — discovers temporal abstractions via higher-order non-causal sequence model controlling base autoregressive model's residual stream. Each controller executes behaviorally meaningful action sequences with learned termination conditions. Operates in fundamentally different paradigm (LLM-style autoregressive models, not world-model imagination), but demonstrates that temporal abstraction benefits from hierarchical structure rather than flat dt-conditioning.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: shaj2023mts3\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Multi Time Scale World Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Shaj V., Gholam Zadeh S., Demir O., Douat L.R., Neumann G.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023 (arXiv:2310.18534)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"MTS3 — probabilistic multi-time-scale state space model with fast and slow latent dynamics for variable-rate prediction. Provides efficient inference across temporal scales. Foundational multi-rate dynamics architecture; our work shows that even with proper multi-rate modeling, the downstream actor-critic training in imagination can exploit temporal conditioning for value overestimation — a failure mode MTS3 does not address (it focuses on prediction quality, not policy training).\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: splates2025\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Stable Planning with Temporally Extended Skills\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [SPlaTES authors]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning Journal (RLC 2025)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"SPlaTES — hierarchical MPC with temporally extended skills + abstract skill world model. Skill learning via mutual-information objective yields diverse, predictable, task-related behavior. Demonstrates that stable long-horizon planning requires modeling skill outcomes (temporal abstraction via hierarchy), not primitive-action outcomes at variable time steps — consistent with our finding that flat dt-embedding fails.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: beckerehmck2023multitimestep\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Multi-timestep models for Model-based Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Becker-Ehmck P., Peters J., van der Smagt P.]\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2310.05672\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Proposes multi-timestep training objective (weighted sum of losses at various future horizons) for one-step dynamics models to improve long-horizon R² score. Addresses compounding error at the model training level; our work shows that even with perfect multi-step prediction, the imagination-based actor-critic can still fail due to value overestimation from dt-conditioning — a downstream failure mode distinct from prediction accuracy.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: liu2022dmve\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Dynamic-Horizon Model-Based Value Estimation With Latent Imagination\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Liu Y., et al.]\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"IEEE TNNLS (arXiv:2009.09593)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: background\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"DMVE — adaptive rollout horizon selection via horizon reliability detection (comparing value expansion errors between raw and reconstructed observations). Addresses the horizon selection problem in imagination-based value estimation. Complementary to our work: DMVE selects among fixed-dt horizons; we show that even optimal horizon selection cannot fix the CIVO when dt-embedding is trained (the overestimation is in the value function, not the horizon length).\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# --- Incremental scan synthesis (2026-05-14) ---\\\\n#\\\\n# NOVELTY ASSESSMENT:\\\\n# No paper found that diagnoses dt_embedding → CIVO\\\\n# (value overestimation) → stochastic collapse in imagination-based\\\\n# MBRL. Our core contribution remains novel.\\\\n#\\\\n# TAWM (ICML 2025) is the closest methodological overlap but\\\\n# operates in MPC paradigm and reaches opposite conclusion\\\\n# (Δt conditioning is beneficial). This actually STRENGTHENS\\\\n# our novelty: \\\\\\\"Δt conditioning helps in MPC but hurts in\\\\n# imagination-based training\\\\\\\" is a more nuanced and surprising finding.\\\\n#\\\\n# LANDSCAPE TREND:\\\\n# The field is converging on HIERARCHY (THICK, Hieros, Director,\\\\n# HWM, SPlaTES, MetaController) as the solution to temporal\\\\n# abstraction in world models, avoiding flat variable-dt approaches.\\\\n# Our diagnostic paper explains WHY flat dt approaches fail,\\\\n# providing theoretical grounding for this empirical trend.\\\\n#\\\\n# ACTION ITEMS FOR PAPER:\\\\n# 1. MUST CITE: nhu2025tawm — differentiate MPC vs imagination paradigm\\\\n# 2. SHOULD CITE: li2025lsimagine — concurrent Dreamer horizon extension\\\\n# 3. SHOULD CITE: shaj2023mts3 — multi-rate dynamics foundation\\\\n# 4. CONSIDER: zhang2026hwm — latest hierarchical WM planning\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# INCREMENTAL SCAN (2026-05-15): Value expansion horizon & overestimation\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 4 new papers from targeted search on:\\\\n#   - \\\\\\\"temporal abstraction\\\\\\\" + \\\\\\\"world model\\\\\\\" + RL (2024-2026)\\\\n#   - \\\\\\\"CIVO\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"value overestimation\\\\\\\" + MBRL\\\\n#   - \\\\\\\"imagination horizon\\\\\\\" + world model\\\\n#   - \\\\\\\"value function collapse\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"value divergence\\\\\\\" + MBRL\\\\n#   - variable timestep imagination + actor-critic failure\\\\n#\\\\n# KEY FINDING: Still NO paper diagnosing dt_embedding → CIVO\\\\n# → stochastic collapse in imagination-based MBRL.\\\\n# Core contribution remains novel. Palenicek 2025 is the most\\\\n# relevant new addition — shows even oracle dynamics don't help\\\\n# with longer rollout horizons, supporting our thesis that the\\\\n# bottleneck is NOT model accuracy but value estimation corruption.\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: palenicek2025diminishing\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Diminishing Return of Value Expansion Methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Palenicek D., Lutter M., Carvalho J., Dennert D., Ahmad F., Peters J.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"Reinforcement Learning Journal (RLC 2025, arXiv:2412.20537)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: evaluation\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows longer model-based rollout horizons have diminishing returns in sample efficiency — even with oracle dynamics models, each additional expansion step yields progressively smaller gains. The bottleneck is NOT model accuracy. Directly supports our finding: extending imagination horizon via dt>1 does not improve performance because the problem lies in value estimation (CIVO from dt_embedding), not dynamics quality. Strengthens our argument that dt=1 imagination is sufficient.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 5\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: kim2025deas\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DEAS: DEtached value learning with Action Sequence for Scalable Offline RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Kim C., Seo Y., et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2510.07730\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Shows action sequences (temporal extension via options/semi-MDP) cause excessive value overestimation in actor-critic algorithms; proposes detached value learning where critic trains separately from policy. Parallel to our finding: temporal extension (whether via action sequences or dt-embedding) creates value overestimation in actor-critic; DEAS's detached learning is analogous to our dt=1 imagination fix (decoupling temporal abstraction from value estimation).\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 4\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: luo2025vpl\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Case for Validation Buffer in Pessimistic Actor-Critic\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Nauman M., Ostaszewski M., Cygan M.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"IJCAI 2025 (arXiv:2403.01014)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"Validation Pessimism Learning (VPL) — uses validation buffer to adaptively adjust pessimism levels throughout training; shows pessimism is beneficial only during overestimation, detrimental during underestimation. Relevant to our setting: our trained dt_embedding creates persistent overestimation (nve>1.5x), suggesting adaptive pessimism could be a complementary mitigation to our dt=1 imagination restriction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n- paper_id: lenz2025macura\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Trust the Model Where It Trusts Itself -- Model-Based Actor-Critic with Uncertainty-Aware Rollout Adaption\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Lenz S., et al.]\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv:2405.19014\\\\\\\"\\\\n  category: method-component\\\\n  role: \\\\\\\"MACURA — uses model uncertainty to adaptively determine local rollout length in model-based actor-critic; avoids uniform rollout horizon. Addresses rollout quality from the uncertainty perspective; our work identifies a different mechanism: even if the model is confident, dt-embedding-conditioned imagination can produce overestimated value estimates (CIVO). MACURA's uncertainty-based termination would not catch our failure mode because the model predictions may be accurate while value estimates are corrupted.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  relevance_score: 3\\\\n\\\\n# --- Incremental scan synthesis (2026-05-15) ---\\\\n#\\\\n# NOVELTY ASSESSMENT (confirmed):\\\\n# No paper found that diagnoses dt_embedding → CIVO\\\\n# (value overestimation) → stochastic collapse in imagination-based\\\\n# MBRL. Core contribution remains novel after 2nd scan.\\\\n#\\\\n# KEY NEW INSIGHT from Palenicek 2025:\\\\n# Even oracle dynamics models show diminishing returns with longer\\\\n# rollouts. This means the value estimation bottleneck (not model\\\\n# accuracy) is increasingly recognized as the fundamental limit\\\\n# of imagination-based MBRL — our paper explains a specific\\\\n# mechanism (dt_embedding-induced CIVO) by which\\\\n# this bottleneck manifests.\\\\n#\\\\n# DEAS (Kim 2025) provides independent evidence from offline RL\\\\n# that temporal extension (action sequences) → value overestimation\\\\n# in actor-critic. Different setting (offline vs online, action\\\\n# sequences vs dt-embedding) but same causal pattern.\\\\n#\\\\n# NO DIRECT COMPETITORS FOUND at CoRL 2025/2026.\\\\n#\\\\n# UPDATED ACTION ITEMS FOR PAPER:\\\\n# 1. MUST CITE: palenicek2025diminishing — rollout horizon diminishing\\\\n#    returns support our \\\\\\\"dt=1 is sufficient\\\\\\\" conclusion\\\\n# 2. SHOULD CITE: kim2025deas — independent evidence of temporal\\\\n#    extension → value overestimation in actor-critic\\\\n# 3. CONSIDER: luo2025vpl — adaptive pessimism as potential mitigation\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 55491, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUAMbvnaUBshfhPiBxTZbA","session_id":"2c57719a-f24e-4bf2-bbe7-05d690719237","uuid":"7f214745-e05c-41c5-b38a-804260fce7f1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:01:45.352Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B7LzkTMgKNGQzNtMhoXxvC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Analysis \\& Discussion}\n2\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n3\t\n4\tThe preceding results establish that the combination of trained $\\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure is the causal factor behind pathological value dynamics in variable-rate DreamerV3. In this section, we synthesize these findings into a mechanistic account of conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO), explain why the same CIVO trigger produces divergent outcomes across seeds, contrast the imagination-based setting with MPC-based temporal conditioning, and discuss the fundamental tension inherent in the $\\dt$-embedding design.\n5\t\n6\t\\subsection{The CIVO Mechanism}\n7\t\\label{sec:civo_mechanism}\n8\t\n9\tThe RSSM fidelity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}) reveals the mechanistic core of CIVO.  The trained $\\dt$-embedding enables the reward head to learn temporally differentiated predictions, but these predictions are asymmetrically accurate: reward MAE at $\\dt{=}1$ is $0.0568$, while at $\\dt{=}2$ it rises to $0.1579$---a $2.78\\times$ gap.  This gap is not an artifact of prediction difficulty alone; the no-embedding configuration (008f) exhibits a $1.01\\times$ gap, and the baseline shows $1.56\\times$.  The trained embedding actively amplifies the fidelity discrepancy by allowing the reward head to co-adapt with the temporal signal.\n10\t\n11\tThis asymmetric fidelity initiates a feedback loop with four stages: (1) the trained $\\dt$-embedding causes RSSM predictions at $\\dt > 1$ to carry systematically biased reward estimates; (2) the critic, trained entirely on imagined trajectories, internalizes these biased rewards as optimistic value estimates; (3) the actor exploits the overestimated regions of the value landscape, shifting its policy toward states that appear high-value only under the biased model; and (4) this policy shift further skews the distribution of imagined states, reinforcing the bias.  The diagnostic signature is a sustained $\\nve$ above $1.5\\times$, followed by evaluation collapse.\n12\t\n13\tA random-policy control experiment ($N{=}100$ rollouts, 95\\% bootstrap CI) decomposes CIVO into two necessary stages.  \\textbf{Stage~1 (dynamics bias):} even under a random policy, $\\dt{=}2$ imagination produces $1.68\\times$ higher mean reward than $\\dt{=}1$ in the trained-embedding model (008d)---the RSSM prior already generates reward-favorable trajectories at $\\dt > 1$, independent of actor behavior.  \\textbf{Stage~2 (actor exploitation):} the trained actor learns to exploit these biased regions, amplifying the ratio to $2.90\\times$.  In the no-embedding control (008f), neither stage is present: the random-policy ratio is $1.11\\times$ and the trained-actor ratio is $0.98\\times$.  The decomposition attributes $\\approx 36\\%$ of the total effect to dynamics bias and $\\approx 64\\%$ to actor exploitation, confirming that both stages are necessary for CIVO to manifest.\n14\t\n15\tThis mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional}, but differs in origin: delusional bias arises from policy-class constraints, while CIVO is model-induced---the reward head's co-adaptation with the temporal embedding produces systematically upward-biased predictions at $\\dt > 1$.  The consequence (policy exploitation of overestimated regions) is similar, but the causal pathway is distinct.\n16\t\n17\t\\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping and Gradient Decomposition}\n18\t\\label{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}\n19\t\n20\t\\subsubsection{Design and Hypothesis}\n21\t\\label{sec:sgs_design}\n22\t\n23\tThe preceding analysis establishes that a trained temporal embedding $e_{\\Delta t}$ is necessary for CIVO (Section~\\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}). We next ask \\textit{which gradient pathway} through $e_{\\Delta t}$ amplifies the overestimation. In DreamerV3, the temporal embedding conditions all prediction heads simultaneously through the RSSM latent state. A natural hypothesis is that the \\textit{reward head}---which directly supplies $\\lambda$-return targets to the critic---is the primary amplifier: if reward predictions develop $\\Delta t$-dependent biases, the critic inherits and compounds these biases across the $H$-step imagination rollout.\n24\t\n25\tTo test this, we introduce \\textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): during the backward pass, $e_{\\Delta t}$ is detached before entering the reward head,\n26\t$$\\hat{r}_t = f_{\\text{reward}}\\big(\\operatorname{sg}(e_{\\Delta t}),\\; h_t,\\; z_t\\big),$$\n27\twhere $\\operatorname{sg}(\\cdot)$ denotes the stop-gradient operator. This prevents reward-loss gradients from updating $e_{\\Delta t}$'s parameters while preserving the embedding's forward-pass contribution. All other gradient pathways---observation decoders, continuation predictor, KL dynamics---remain intact. We apply this intervention to the primary CIVO-exhibiting configuration: Walker Walk, trained $e_{\\Delta t}$ ($d{=}32$), subsampling enabled, $H{=}8$, $\\Delta t{=}2$ imagination (exp\\_016e; identical to exp\\_008d except for \\texttt{reward\\_dt\\_emb\\_detach=True}).\n28\t\n29\t\n30\t\\subsubsection{Gradient Decomposition Reveals the Reward Pathway is Inactive}\n31\t\\label{sec:gradient_decomposition}\n32\t\n33\tExp\\_016e collapses in the same qualitative pattern as the unmodified control: peak evaluation 326.7 at 75K steps (exp\\_016e) followed by catastrophic decline to 31.7 at 95K, with NVE reaching $10.42\\times$ (exp\\_016e). The SGS intervention neither prevented nor attenuated CIVO.\n34\t\n35\tTo explain this null result, we performed a gradient decomposition analysis using \\texttt{torch.autograd.grad} to measure each loss component's contribution to $\\nabla_\\theta e_{\\Delta t}$, where $\\theta$ denotes the temporal embedding parameters (D061). Table~\\ref{tab:gradient_decomposition} reports the results.\n36\t\n37\t\\begin{table}[h]\n38\t\\centering\n39\t\\caption{Gradient contributions to the temporal embedding $e_{\\Delta t}$ by loss component, measured via \\texttt{torch.autograd.grad} at step 95K (D061). Gradient norms are $\\ell_2$ norms of $\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L}_i$ for each loss $\\mathcal{L}_i$.}\n40\t\\label{tab:gradient_decomposition}\n41\t\\small\n42\t\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n43\t\\toprule\n44\tLoss component & $\\|\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L}_i\\|_2$ & Share (\\%) \\\\\n45\t\\midrule\n46\tObservation decoders (orientations, velocity, height) & 1.505 & 92.7 \\\\\n47\tKL dynamics & 0.119 & 7.3 \\\\\n48\tContinuation head & $<$0.001 & $<$0.1 \\\\\n49\tReward head & 0.000 & 0.0 \\\\\n50\t\\midrule\n51\t\\textbf{Total} & \\textbf{1.624} & \\textbf{100} \\\\\n52\t\\bottomrule\n53\t\\end{tabular}\n54\t\\end{table}\n55\t\n56\tThe reward head contributes \\textit{numerically negligible---zero to within floating-point precision} gradient to the temporal embedding. Exp\\_016e's stop-gradient intervention detaches a gradient pathway that carries no signal---it is an architectural no-op. The null result is therefore expected: the intervention removes nothing.\n57\t\n58\tThe zero reward gradient is a consequence of the relative output dimensionality. All decoder losses backpropagate through the same RSSM latent state to reach $e_{\\Delta t}$. The observation decoders reconstruct a 24-dimensional proprioceptive signal (14 orientations, 9 velocities, 1 height), while the reward head predicts a single scalar. This ${\\sim}24\\!\\times$ dimensionality asymmetry, compounded by DreamerV3's symlog reward scaling which further compresses the reward gradient magnitude, renders the reward head's contribution numerically negligible---zero to within floating-point precision.\n59\t\n60\t\n61\t\\subsubsection{Implications for the CIVO Mechanism}\n62\t\\label{sec:sgs_implications}\n63\t\n64\tThe gradient decomposition reframes the mechanistic understanding of how the temporal embedding drives CIVO.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Observation reconstruction dominates temporal embedding learning.} The temporal embedding's parameters are shaped almost entirely (93\\%) by the observation reconstruction objective (D061). This means $e_{\\Delta t}$ learns to encode temporal information that helps minimize \\textit{observation prediction error}, not reward prediction error. If this learned representation introduces systematic biases in latent state predictions at $\\Delta t{>}1$---for instance, by encouraging the RSSM to produce states that are easy to decode but dynamically inaccurate---these biases propagate to \\textit{all} downstream computation, including reward prediction and the critic's value estimates. CIVO thus originates in the observation reconstruction objective's influence on latent dynamics, not in reward-specific pathways.\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{Reframing the intervention target.} Any gradient-level strategy that targets the reward head alone---reward prediction regularization, reward-conditioned value clipping, or the reward-only SGS tested here---addresses $<$1\\% of the gradient flow to $e_{\\Delta t}$ and cannot attenuate CIVO. Effective gradient-level interventions must address the dominant observation decoder pathway. This motivates the \\textit{full-pathway SGS} design (exp\\_018): detaching $e_{\\Delta t}$ from all decoder and reward gradients, retaining only the 7\\% KL dynamics contribution. Full-pathway SGS removes 93\\% of the gradient to $e_{\\Delta t}$, constituting a genuine gradient-level intervention rather than the null operation of reward-only SGS.\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Connection to RSSM fidelity analysis.} The observation-dominated gradient structure is consistent with the RSSM fidelity results in Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}: the trained-embedding condition (exp\\_008d) shows a $2.78\\times$ reward gap between $\\Delta t{=}1$ and $\\Delta t{=}2$ transitions, despite the reward head receiving zero gradient from $e_{\\Delta t}$. This confirms that the reward head's $\\Delta t$-dependent biases are inherited \\textit{through the latent state}---which is shaped by observation reconstruction gradients---rather than learned directly from the reward objective.\n71\t\n72\t\n73\t\\subsubsection{Exp\\_016e Reinterpreted as Stochastic Variation}\n74\t\\label{sec:016e_reinterp}\n75\t\n76\tWith the gradient decomposition establishing that exp\\_016e is a no-op intervention, the apparent timing differences between exp\\_016e and exp\\_008d---later collapse onset (75K vs.\\ 25K) and higher peak evaluation (326.7 vs.\\ 167.2)---must be attributed to stochastic variation rather than SGS effects. This attribution is consistent with the seed-to-seed variability already observed within the trained-embedding condition: exp\\_009b (same configuration as exp\\_008d, seed 2) reaches peak evaluation 278.5 at 95K (exp\\_009b) with transient NVE of $1.62\\times$ at 45K before self-correcting, compared to exp\\_008d's monotonic collapse from 25K. The qualitative diversity between exp\\_008d (early collapse), exp\\_009b (self-correction), and exp\\_016e (delayed collapse) falls within the range of stochastic outcomes for a single trained-$e_{\\Delta t}$ configuration.\n77\t\n78\tAll three trajectories share the invariant identified by the NVE diagnostic: each crosses the $1.5\\times$ pathological threshold at some point during training (exp\\_008d at 35K: $1.71\\times$; exp\\_009b at 45K: $1.62\\times$; exp\\_016e at 95K: $10.42\\times$). Whether this pathological overestimation leads to sustained collapse or transient recovery depends on early training stochasticity, not on the reward-only SGS intervention (D061).\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{Three Post-CIVO Outcomes: Why Seeds Diverge}\n81\t\\label{sec:seed_divergence}\n82\t\n83\tThe three post-CIVO outcomes (Table~\\ref{tab:post_civo})---full collapse, transient CIVO, and oscillatory degradation---all cross the $\\nve > 1.5\\times$ threshold, yet the downstream damage is stochastic.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \\citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed when CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate (seed~0); if sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb transient overestimation and recover (seed~2).  The oscillatory pattern (seed~1, 009e) represents an intermediate regime: the value function rises then reverses while evaluation oscillates, and entropy remains elevated---suggesting the actor has not fully committed to the overestimated policy but cannot escape it.  This partial capacity loss allows the critic to respond to new data without fully correcting its biases.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}\n86\t\\label{sec:mpc_immune}\n87\t\n88\t\\citet{nhu2025tawm} show that $\\dt$-conditioning benefits TD-MPC2, raising the question of why it fails in DreamerV3.  The critical difference is that MPC re-plans from real states at every step---imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories.  In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\\dt > 1$ is amplified through the closed actor-critic loop.  In terms of the two-stage decomposition (\\S\\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}), MPC is immune because Stage~2 cannot occur: without imagination-based bootstrapping, the actor cannot exploit the dynamics bias.  This aligns with \\citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts}, who find that rollout quality dominates model-based RL performance, and with \\citet{tallec2019making}'s analysis of how temporal resolution mismatches destabilize value learning.\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{The \\texorpdfstring{$\\dt$}{dt}-Embedding Dilemma}\n91\t\\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}\n92\t\n93\tThe factorial results reveal a fundamental tension: removing the $\\dt$-embedding (008f) eliminates CIVO but at a $13\\times$ cost to reward prediction quality ($0.7469$ vs.\\ $0.0568$ MAE)---the world model cannot distinguish temporal scales.  Restricting imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (008e\\_v3) prevents CIVO in 1 of 2 seeds but the other collapses via Pathway~B (\\S\\ref{sec:f6}).  Neither approach alone provides a complete solution.\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}\n96\t\\label{sec:pathways}\n97\t\n98\tThe two collapse mechanisms identified in \\S\\ref{sec:f6}---CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing)---have distinct diagnostic signatures but a shared root cause: the trained $\\dt$-embedding's learned features affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B).  This explains why $\\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\\times$ for 008e\\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse.  The existence of multiple pathways suggests that mitigation strategies focused exclusively on the imagination channel---restricting $\\dt$, truncating rollouts, or smoothing rewards \\citep{lee2024dreamsmooth}---may be insufficient.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}\n101\t\\label{sec:implications}\n102\t\n103\tCIVO identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training: the RSSM cannot maintain prediction fidelity across temporal resolutions when the embedding co-adapts with the reward head.  Hierarchical approaches---THICK \\citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \\citep{mattes2024hieros}, LS-Imagine \\citep{li2025lsimagine}---may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale.  Whether CIVO transfers to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4; \\citealt{hafner2025dreamerv4}) depends on whether attention weights co-adapt with temporal embeddings analogously.  Reward-only gradient stopping is an architectural no-op because the reward head contributes zero gradient to $e_{\\Delta t}$ (\\S\\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}); full-pathway SGS that detaches the dominant observation-decoder gradients constitutes a genuine intervention targeting 93\\% of the gradient flow (exp\\_018; \\S\\ref{sec:sgs_implications}).  Whether spectral normalization or other regularization strategies can preserve temporal fidelity while preventing co-adaptation remains an open question.\n104\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FXrjvQWSFc6K3k2oYyLyeD","session_id":"2c57719a-f24e-4bf2-bbe7-05d690719237","uuid":"0bd5b5b3-4606-41ff-bf62-8fc81c35ae60","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:01:44.973Z"}
