{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab8e43eaaa2cd1c67","tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUAMbvnaUBshfhPiBxTZbA","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":81740,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":154475},"uuid":"8189f39e-242e-4e27-901c-06425c9c79a5","session_id":"2c57719a-f24e-4bf2-bbe7-05d690719237"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUAMbvnaUBshfhPiBxTZbA","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review as Reviewer R2, focusing on Novelty and Related Work.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper identifies Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO) as a failure mode when adding dt-conditioning to DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic training. Through a factorial ablation on Walker Walk, the authors isolate the combination of a trained dt-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the joint necessary trigger. They further discover that CIVO is one of multiple vulnerability pathways created by the trained embedding, and contrast their negative result with TAWM's success in MPC, attributing the difference to feedback architecture (bootstrapping vs. re-planning).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The TAWM contrast is the paper's strongest novelty claim.** The finding that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination-based actor-critic is genuinely surprising and non-obvious. This is not merely \"model-based RL has overestimation problems\" -- it is a precise architectural diagnosis of *why* the same conditioning mechanism produces opposite outcomes in two paradigms. This contrast elevates the paper above a standard negative-result report.\n\n- **The gradient decomposition (93%/7%/0%) is a novel diagnostic contribution.** No prior work has quantitatively decomposed gradient flow through a temporal embedding in a Dreamer-family world model. The finding that the reward head contributes exactly 0% gradient to the dt-embedding -- making reward-only SGS an architectural no-op -- is a concrete, falsifiable result that corrects a natural but wrong intuition about where the vulnerability lies. The connection to HarmonyDream (observation loss dominates reward loss) is acknowledged but the backward-pass decomposition goes further.\n\n- **The factorial design is methodologically rigorous for a single-environment study.** The 2x2 causal matrix (trained/frozen/removed x exposed/unexposed) cleanly isolates the joint necessary condition. The paper correctly identifies that neither factor alone is sufficient, which is a stronger claim than typical ablations that test factors independently.\n\n- **Honest reporting of partial falsification.** The paper transparently reports that C6 (dt=1 imagination as mitigation) was falsified, that the NVE diagnostic has a false positive, and that collapse is stochastic. This candor is unusual and valuable.\n\n- **The dual-pathway taxonomy (Pathway A: value-driven/entropy-decreasing vs. Pathway B: eval-driven/entropy-increasing) is a useful conceptual contribution**, even if the evidence base is thin (1 seed each).\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1: Single-environment scope severely limits novelty claims.** The entire empirical analysis is conducted on Walker Walk. The paper acknowledges this but the limitation is more damaging than the authors seem to appreciate. Walker Walk is a relatively simple locomotion task with smooth, dense rewards -- precisely the kind of environment where value overestimation effects are most visible. The paper cannot distinguish whether CIVO is a general vulnerability of imagination-based dt-conditioning or a Walker Walk-specific artifact. The abstract and introduction make claims about \"imagination-based actor-critic architectures\" broadly, but the evidence supports only claims about \"DreamerV3 on Walker Walk.\" The dream report version claims cross-environment results (4 environments, 22 seeds) that the submitted paper does not contain -- this gap must be addressed before acceptance.\n\n**W2: The novelty of identifying value overestimation in MBRL is incremental.** The general phenomenon -- that model errors compound through imagination-based training and produce value overestimation -- is well-established (Janner et al. 2019, Frauenknecht et al. 2025, Barkley & Fridovich-Keil 2024). The specific contribution is narrowing this to the dt-embedding channel, but the causal chain (biased model predictions -> critic overestimation -> actor exploitation) is the standard MBRL exploitation story. The dt-embedding is one more instance of the known pattern: any learned conditioning that amplifies model error in imagination will produce overestimation. The paper could strengthen novelty by articulating more precisely what is *structurally different* about dt-conditioning versus other forms of model exploitation (e.g., why wouldn't any auxiliary embedding create the same problem?).\n\n**W3: The NVE diagnostic is underpowered and unlikely to generalize.** With n=8 seeds in a single environment, the 7/8 classification (AUROC=0.933) has confidence intervals so wide (Clopper-Pearson 95% CI [47.3%, 99.7%] on accuracy) that the result is essentially uninformative statistically. The paper appropriately notes this in the appendix but the main text still positions the NVE threshold as a \"diagnostic framework.\" A diagnostic that has been validated on 8 data points from one environment and one architecture is not a framework -- it is an observation. The threshold value (1.5x) and window (35K+) are almost certainly task-specific (reward scale, training dynamics).\n\n**W4: The \"multiple vulnerability pathways\" claim rests on a single seed.** Pathway B (non-CIVO collapse) is observed in exactly one seed (008e_v3_s2). While the contrast with Pathway A is qualitatively compelling (opposite entropy dynamics), a single observation cannot establish a \"pathway.\" It could be an outlier, a bug, or a phenomenon specific to the interaction of this particular random initialization with the embedding architecture. The claim would require at minimum 3-5 seeds exhibiting Pathway B to be credible as a distinct failure mode rather than an anecdote.\n\n**W5: Missing discussion of why hierarchical approaches would avoid CIVO.** The paper repeatedly points to THICK, Hieros, and LS-Imagine as alternatives that \"may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale\" (Section 6.6). But this is speculation without analysis. Could hierarchical approaches not suffer analogous overestimation if their high-level value functions are trained on imagined trajectories? The paper does not analyze what structural property of hierarchy prevents the CIVO feedback loop. This weakens the \"actionable guidance\" claim.\n\n**W6: The TAWM comparison lacks depth.** The paper identifies the MPC vs. imagination distinction as the critical factor, but the analysis is purely qualitative. TAWM also uses a critic (TD-MPC2 has a learned value function); the distinction is that TD-MPC2's critic is trained on real transitions via TD learning, not on imagined trajectories. The paper should be more precise: is the claim that *any* critic trained partly on imagined trajectories would exhibit CIVO? What about Dyna-style methods that mix real and imagined data? The MPC/imagination binary is too coarse.\n\n**W7: No comparison to standard overestimation mitigations.** The paper does not test whether well-known overestimation countermeasures -- clipped double-Q (TD3), pessimistic value estimates (MOPO), ensemble disagreement, or simply reducing the imagination horizon -- would mitigate CIVO. The literature map lists these works but the paper only tests SGS variants and dt=1 restriction. Testing at least one standard mitigation would significantly strengthen the analysis by showing whether CIVO is a novel failure mode requiring novel solutions or simply an instance of known overestimation amenable to known fixes.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you tested any configuration on a second environment (e.g., Cheetah Run, which appears in your experiment schedule)? The absence of cross-environment results is the single largest weakness.\n\n2. The 2.78x reward gap in 008d -- is this measured at a specific checkpoint or averaged over training? How does this gap evolve over training? Does it precede the NVE spike or co-evolve?\n\n3. For the TAWM comparison: TD-MPC2 also has a learned value function trained via TD learning. Can you be more precise about which property of TD-MPC2's value training prevents CIVO? Is it the use of real states as bootstrapping targets, the shorter effective planning horizon, or something else?\n\n4. The gradient decomposition is measured at step 95K (a single checkpoint). Is this stable across training or does the gradient distribution shift? Early in training, before CIVO manifests, is the reward gradient still zero?\n\n5. Have you verified that the 1.5x NVE threshold would detect CIVO if the reward scale were different (e.g., sparse rewards, reward shaping)?\n\n6. Could you run exp_008d with clipped double-Q critics (a la TD3) to test whether standard overestimation mitigations address CIVO?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\nThe paper's related work section is compact (3 paragraphs) but covers the most critical references. The literature map (76 papers) is extensive, though most are not cited in the paper. Specific gaps in the submitted paper:\n\n1. **TD(lambda) and multi-step returns.** The paper discusses variable-dt imagination but never connects to the classical multi-step return literature. Sutton (1988) and the eligibility trace framework directly address how temporal scale affects value estimation. The n-step return bias-variance tradeoff is directly relevant to why dt>1 imagination produces overestimation.\n\n2. **REDQ (Chen et al., 2021).** Randomized Ensemble Double Q-learning addresses overestimation through ensemble subsampling. Relevant as a potential mitigation and as context for the overestimation discussion.\n\n3. **Reward shaping sensitivity.** The paper identifies reward nonlinearity as a CIVO condition (in the paper framing document, though not in the submitted paper). Ng et al. (1999) on potential-based reward shaping and the broader reward function sensitivity literature (e.g., reward hacking in RLHF) could contextualize why the reward prediction gap matters.\n\n4. **Negative results papers in ML.** The paper positions itself as a \"diagnostic analysis\" but does not cite the growing body of negative-results papers. Sculley et al. (2018, \"Winner's Curse\"), Lipton & Steinhardt (2019, \"Troubling Trends\"), or community norms around reporting negative results would help position the contribution genre.\n\n5. **DreamerV3's symlog transform.** The paper mentions symlog scaling compresses reward gradients (contributing to the 0% reward gradient finding) but does not discuss whether symlog is a contributing factor to CIVO vs. a protective factor. This is architecturally specific and worth discussing.\n\n6. **Palenicek et al. (2025) \"Diminishing Return of Value Expansion Methods\"** is listed in the literature map as relevance 5 but not cited in the paper. This work directly supports the finding that extending imagination horizon via dt>1 does not improve performance.\n\n7. **Kim et al. (2025) DEAS** provides independent evidence from offline RL that temporal extension causes value overestimation in actor-critic. Listed in the literature map but not cited.\n\n### Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n### Confidence: 4 (High)\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper identifies a real and interesting phenomenon (CIVO), conducts a methodologically careful factorial analysis, and makes the valuable observation that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based training. The gradient decomposition is a genuinely novel contribution. However, the single-environment scope is a critical limitation that prevents the findings from supporting the generality implied by the framing. The NVE diagnostic is statistically underpowered (n=8, CI includes chance). The \"multiple pathways\" claim rests on a single seed. The novelty relative to the broader MBRL overestimation literature is incremental -- the specific mechanism (dt-embedding) is new, but the causal pattern (biased model -> overestimated values -> actor exploitation) is well-established. For a top venue with ~25% acceptance, the paper needs at minimum: (a) one additional environment demonstrating CIVO generalizes, (b) comparison to at least one standard overestimation mitigation, and (c) more seeds for the Pathway B claim. In its current form, the findings are an interesting workshop contribution or a strong appendix to a future methods paper, but the evidence base is too narrow for a main-conference diagnostic paper at a top venue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab8e43eaaa2cd1c67 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab8e43eaaa2cd1c67' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 84099\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 154477</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2c57719a-f24e-4bf2-bbe7-05d690719237","uuid":"3e04ba87-178b-4b9a-94b4-e8dbdc323dad","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:03:54.573Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"When Does Temporal Abstraction Fail in World-Model RL? Reward Landscape Geometry Determines Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation\"\n\nThe paper investigates Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO) — a failure mode in world-model RL where trained temporal embeddings (Δt-conditioning) amplify RSSM prediction errors into pathological critic overestimation. Through cross-environment experiments on DreamerV3 with Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop, the paper finds CIVO requires specific task properties: (1) nonlinear reward functions (reward gap > 2×), (2) high-dimensional observations for embedding gradient amplification, (3) smooth reward landscapes. Walker Walk is the only environment where CIVO manifests (NVE peak 2.59×, 100% pathological overestimation, 50% collapse). The NVE>1.5× diagnostic achieves AUROC 1.0 within Walker but degrades to 0.58 cross-environment. The paper positions itself as a \"cautionary tale\" about benchmark-specific findings in MBRL.\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages)\n1. Introduction — Motivates temporal abstraction in world-model RL, introduces CIVO, states environment-specificity as central finding\n2. Background — DreamerV3, variable-rate imagination, MPC vs actor-critic comparison (references TAWM Nhu et al. 2025)\n3. Method — Experimental design (4 DMC tasks, dt-embedding configs), NVE metric, reward gap, embedding separation rate\n4. Experiments — Walker Walk (complete case study), Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop, cross-env summary, mechanistic analysis (RSSM fidelity, embedding dynamics, entropy, value calibration), NVE diagnostic validity, intervention analysis (SGS variants, uncertainty-gated truncation), TAWM comparison\n5. Discussion — Boundary conditions for CIVO (3 necessary conditions), multiple vulnerability pathways, implications for benchmark-specific claims\n6. Conclusion — 4 key findings\n\n## References in the paper (8 total):\n- Hafner et al. 2023 — DreamerV3\n- Nhu et al. 2025 — TAWM (temporal abstraction in world models)\n- Lu et al. 2018 — Non-delusional Q-learning\n- Sutton et al. 1999 — Options framework (temporal abstraction)\n- Vezhnevets et al. 2017 — FeUDal Networks\n- Agarwal et al. 2021 — Statistical precipice (evaluation methodology)\n- Janner et al. 2019 — MBPO (when to trust your model)\n- Henderson et al. 2018 — Deep RL that matters\n\n## Key Claims\n1. CIVO is a real failure mode in temporal abstraction for actor-critic world-model RL\n2. CIVO is environment-specific, requiring: reward nonlinearity (>2× gap), high obs dimensionality, smooth reward landscapes\n3. Diagnostic tools (NVE>1.5×) developed on single environments fail to generalize\n4. Interventions preventing CIVO (frozen embedding, full SGS) also destroy learning\n5. MPC robustness (TAWM) stems from planning horizon truncation, not architecture per se\n6. The broader lesson: benchmark-specific claims in MBRL must be bounded by task properties\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the CIVO mechanism identification? How novel is the \"boundary conditions\" finding? Is this incremental or significant?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key related works missing? How well does the paper situate itself?\n3. **Positioning and framing**: Is \"cautionary tale\" the right framing? Does the paper overclaim or underclaim?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Would the community benefit from these findings? Is there actionable insight?\n5. **Writing quality**: Clarity, organization, presentation\n6. **Comparison to prior work**: Is the distinction from TAWM well-motivated? Are other value overestimation works discussed?\n\nConsider these areas of related work that might be missing:\n- Value overestimation in RL more broadly (TD3, SAC clipped double-Q, REDQ)\n- Model error and compounding in MBRL (Janner et al. 2019 is cited but superficially)\n- Delusional bias literature (Lu et al. 2018 is cited)\n- Temporal difference learning at multiple scales (TD(λ), multi-step returns)\n- Other world model architectures (TransDreamer, IRIS, etc.)\n- Reward shaping / reward function sensitivity literature\n- DMC benchmark sensitivity studies\n- Negative results papers in ML\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing related work\n- Overall score (1-6) with confidence (1-5)\n- Short justification for score\n","agentId":"ab8e43eaaa2cd1c67","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review as Reviewer R2, focusing on Novelty and Related Work.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper identifies Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO) as a failure mode when adding dt-conditioning to DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic training. Through a factorial ablation on Walker Walk, the authors isolate the combination of a trained dt-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the joint necessary trigger. They further discover that CIVO is one of multiple vulnerability pathways created by the trained embedding, and contrast their negative result with TAWM's success in MPC, attributing the difference to feedback architecture (bootstrapping vs. re-planning).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The TAWM contrast is the paper's strongest novelty claim.** The finding that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination-based actor-critic is genuinely surprising and non-obvious. This is not merely \"model-based RL has overestimation problems\" -- it is a precise architectural diagnosis of *why* the same conditioning mechanism produces opposite outcomes in two paradigms. This contrast elevates the paper above a standard negative-result report.\n\n- **The gradient decomposition (93%/7%/0%) is a novel diagnostic contribution.** No prior work has quantitatively decomposed gradient flow through a temporal embedding in a Dreamer-family world model. The finding that the reward head contributes exactly 0% gradient to the dt-embedding -- making reward-only SGS an architectural no-op -- is a concrete, falsifiable result that corrects a natural but wrong intuition about where the vulnerability lies. The connection to HarmonyDream (observation loss dominates reward loss) is acknowledged but the backward-pass decomposition goes further.\n\n- **The factorial design is methodologically rigorous for a single-environment study.** The 2x2 causal matrix (trained/frozen/removed x exposed/unexposed) cleanly isolates the joint necessary condition. The paper correctly identifies that neither factor alone is sufficient, which is a stronger claim than typical ablations that test factors independently.\n\n- **Honest reporting of partial falsification.** The paper transparently reports that C6 (dt=1 imagination as mitigation) was falsified, that the NVE diagnostic has a false positive, and that collapse is stochastic. This candor is unusual and valuable.\n\n- **The dual-pathway taxonomy (Pathway A: value-driven/entropy-decreasing vs. Pathway B: eval-driven/entropy-increasing) is a useful conceptual contribution**, even if the evidence base is thin (1 seed each).\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1: Single-environment scope severely limits novelty claims.** The entire empirical analysis is conducted on Walker Walk. The paper acknowledges this but the limitation is more damaging than the authors seem to appreciate. Walker Walk is a relatively simple locomotion task with smooth, dense rewards -- precisely the kind of environment where value overestimation effects are most visible. The paper cannot distinguish whether CIVO is a general vulnerability of imagination-based dt-conditioning or a Walker Walk-specific artifact. The abstract and introduction make claims about \"imagination-based actor-critic architectures\" broadly, but the evidence supports only claims about \"DreamerV3 on Walker Walk.\" The dream report version claims cross-environment results (4 environments, 22 seeds) that the submitted paper does not contain -- this gap must be addressed before acceptance.\n\n**W2: The novelty of identifying value overestimation in MBRL is incremental.** The general phenomenon -- that model errors compound through imagination-based training and produce value overestimation -- is well-established (Janner et al. 2019, Frauenknecht et al. 2025, Barkley & Fridovich-Keil 2024). The specific contribution is narrowing this to the dt-embedding channel, but the causal chain (biased model predictions -> critic overestimation -> actor exploitation) is the standard MBRL exploitation story. The dt-embedding is one more instance of the known pattern: any learned conditioning that amplifies model error in imagination will produce overestimation. The paper could strengthen novelty by articulating more precisely what is *structurally different* about dt-conditioning versus other forms of model exploitation (e.g., why wouldn't any auxiliary embedding create the same problem?).\n\n**W3: The NVE diagnostic is underpowered and unlikely to generalize.** With n=8 seeds in a single environment, the 7/8 classification (AUROC=0.933) has confidence intervals so wide (Clopper-Pearson 95% CI [47.3%, 99.7%] on accuracy) that the result is essentially uninformative statistically. The paper appropriately notes this in the appendix but the main text still positions the NVE threshold as a \"diagnostic framework.\" A diagnostic that has been validated on 8 data points from one environment and one architecture is not a framework -- it is an observation. The threshold value (1.5x) and window (35K+) are almost certainly task-specific (reward scale, training dynamics).\n\n**W4: The \"multiple vulnerability pathways\" claim rests on a single seed.** Pathway B (non-CIVO collapse) is observed in exactly one seed (008e_v3_s2). While the contrast with Pathway A is qualitatively compelling (opposite entropy dynamics), a single observation cannot establish a \"pathway.\" It could be an outlier, a bug, or a phenomenon specific to the interaction of this particular random initialization with the embedding architecture. The claim would require at minimum 3-5 seeds exhibiting Pathway B to be credible as a distinct failure mode rather than an anecdote.\n\n**W5: Missing discussion of why hierarchical approaches would avoid CIVO.** The paper repeatedly points to THICK, Hieros, and LS-Imagine as alternatives that \"may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale\" (Section 6.6). But this is speculation without analysis. Could hierarchical approaches not suffer analogous overestimation if their high-level value functions are trained on imagined trajectories? The paper does not analyze what structural property of hierarchy prevents the CIVO feedback loop. This weakens the \"actionable guidance\" claim.\n\n**W6: The TAWM comparison lacks depth.** The paper identifies the MPC vs. imagination distinction as the critical factor, but the analysis is purely qualitative. TAWM also uses a critic (TD-MPC2 has a learned value function); the distinction is that TD-MPC2's critic is trained on real transitions via TD learning, not on imagined trajectories. The paper should be more precise: is the claim that *any* critic trained partly on imagined trajectories would exhibit CIVO? What about Dyna-style methods that mix real and imagined data? The MPC/imagination binary is too coarse.\n\n**W7: No comparison to standard overestimation mitigations.** The paper does not test whether well-known overestimation countermeasures -- clipped double-Q (TD3), pessimistic value estimates (MOPO), ensemble disagreement, or simply reducing the imagination horizon -- would mitigate CIVO. The literature map lists these works but the paper only tests SGS variants and dt=1 restriction. Testing at least one standard mitigation would significantly strengthen the analysis by showing whether CIVO is a novel failure mode requiring novel solutions or simply an instance of known overestimation amenable to known fixes.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you tested any configuration on a second environment (e.g., Cheetah Run, which appears in your experiment schedule)? The absence of cross-environment results is the single largest weakness.\n\n2. The 2.78x reward gap in 008d -- is this measured at a specific checkpoint or averaged over training? How does this gap evolve over training? Does it precede the NVE spike or co-evolve?\n\n3. For the TAWM comparison: TD-MPC2 also has a learned value function trained via TD learning. Can you be more precise about which property of TD-MPC2's value training prevents CIVO? Is it the use of real states as bootstrapping targets, the shorter effective planning horizon, or something else?\n\n4. The gradient decomposition is measured at step 95K (a single checkpoint). Is this stable across training or does the gradient distribution shift? Early in training, before CIVO manifests, is the reward gradient still zero?\n\n5. Have you verified that the 1.5x NVE threshold would detect CIVO if the reward scale were different (e.g., sparse rewards, reward shaping)?\n\n6. Could you run exp_008d with clipped double-Q critics (a la TD3) to test whether standard overestimation mitigations address CIVO?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\nThe paper's related work section is compact (3 paragraphs) but covers the most critical references. The literature map (76 papers) is extensive, though most are not cited in the paper. Specific gaps in the submitted paper:\n\n1. **TD(lambda) and multi-step returns.** The paper discusses variable-dt imagination but never connects to the classical multi-step return literature. Sutton (1988) and the eligibility trace framework directly address how temporal scale affects value estimation. The n-step return bias-variance tradeoff is directly relevant to why dt>1 imagination produces overestimation.\n\n2. **REDQ (Chen et al., 2021).** Randomized Ensemble Double Q-learning addresses overestimation through ensemble subsampling. Relevant as a potential mitigation and as context for the overestimation discussion.\n\n3. **Reward shaping sensitivity.** The paper identifies reward nonlinearity as a CIVO condition (in the paper framing document, though not in the submitted paper). Ng et al. (1999) on potential-based reward shaping and the broader reward function sensitivity literature (e.g., reward hacking in RLHF) could contextualize why the reward prediction gap matters.\n\n4. **Negative results papers in ML.** The paper positions itself as a \"diagnostic analysis\" but does not cite the growing body of negative-results papers. Sculley et al. (2018, \"Winner's Curse\"), Lipton & Steinhardt (2019, \"Troubling Trends\"), or community norms around reporting negative results would help position the contribution genre.\n\n5. **DreamerV3's symlog transform.** The paper mentions symlog scaling compresses reward gradients (contributing to the 0% reward gradient finding) but does not discuss whether symlog is a contributing factor to CIVO vs. a protective factor. This is architecturally specific and worth discussing.\n\n6. **Palenicek et al. (2025) \"Diminishing Return of Value Expansion Methods\"** is listed in the literature map as relevance 5 but not cited in the paper. This work directly supports the finding that extending imagination horizon via dt>1 does not improve performance.\n\n7. **Kim et al. (2025) DEAS** provides independent evidence from offline RL that temporal extension causes value overestimation in actor-critic. Listed in the literature map but not cited.\n\n### Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n### Confidence: 4 (High)\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper identifies a real and interesting phenomenon (CIVO), conducts a methodologically careful factorial analysis, and makes the valuable observation that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based training. The gradient decomposition is a genuinely novel contribution. However, the single-environment scope is a critical limitation that prevents the findings from supporting the generality implied by the framing. The NVE diagnostic is statistically underpowered (n=8, CI includes chance). The \"multiple pathways\" claim rests on a single seed. The novelty relative to the broader MBRL overestimation literature is incremental -- the specific mechanism (dt-embedding) is new, but the causal pattern (biased model -> overestimated values -> actor exploitation) is well-established. For a top venue with ~25% acceptance, the paper needs at minimum: (a) one additional environment demonstrating CIVO generalizes, (b) comparison to at least one standard overestimation mitigation, and (c) more seeds for the Pathway B claim. In its current form, the findings are an interesting workshop contribution or a strong appendix to a future methods paper, but the evidence base is too narrow for a main-conference diagnostic paper at a top venue."}],"totalDurationMs":154477,"totalTokens":84099,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_read_input_tokens":80474,"output_tokens":2888,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":734},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2888,"cache_read_input_tokens":80474,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":734,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a6344cb48ea88ebbb","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FXrjvQWSFc6K3k2oYyLyeD","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":79244,"tool_uses":34,"duration_ms":263006},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"b7f300d5-7fbd-4ba1-9830-b0aa2ae1f1e6","session_id":"2c57719a-f24e-4bf2-bbe7-05d690719237"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PS4gQL8iDZTKDzXtGMeCdH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MdvNsZ2165PUzuj5PWwami","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Review: R1 (Methods and Experiments)\n\n**Paper:** \"When Does Temporal Abstraction Fail in World-Model RL? Reward Landscape Geometry Determines Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation\"\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper presents a factorial diagnostic analysis of why variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3, identifying the combination of a trained temporal embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the joint trigger for conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO). The investigation is methodologically careful within its scope, using controlled ablations on Walker Walk to isolate the causal chain from trained dt-embedding through RSSM reward prediction bias to critic overestimation and policy collapse. However, the paper is restricted to a single environment and a single architecture, and the statistical basis for its quantitative claims (n=8 seeds for the NVE diagnostic) is thin.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured factorial design.** The 2x2 causal isolation (trained/frozen/removed embedding x multi-rate/single-rate data) is clean. The 008d vs 008f comparison (identical except dt-embedding) and the 008d vs 008a comparison (identical except data exposure) provide the strongest evidence for the joint-necessity claim. The paper correctly identifies that neither factor alone is sufficient.\n\n- **Gradient decomposition is a genuine contribution.** The finding that 93% of dt-embedding gradients come from observation decoders (0% from the reward head) is non-obvious, reframes the CIVO mechanism as representation-driven rather than reward-driven, and directly explains why the reward-only SGS intervention (exp_016e) is an architectural no-op. This analysis is reproducible, quantitative, and actionable for future work.\n\n- **Honest reporting of partially-falsified claims.** The paper transparently reports that restricting imagination to dt=1 does not prevent collapse (1/2 seeds), identifies the qualitatively distinct Pathway B (eval-driven, entropy increasing), and uses the baseline seed 2 (009a) control to rule out seed fragility. This intellectual honesty elevates the work above papers that would simply bury the inconvenient finding.\n\n- **The dual-pathway finding (Pathway A vs Pathway B) is interesting.** Identifying that trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms -- not just CIVO -- adds diagnostic value beyond a single failure mode characterization.\n\n- **Per-seed trajectory reporting** following Agarwal et al. (2021) rather than aggregated means is appropriate given the stochastic divergence across seeds and the small sample sizes.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1: Single-environment scope is a critical limitation.**\nThe paper's title references \"reward landscape geometry\" and asks \"When Does Temporal Abstraction Fail?\" -- implying cross-environment analysis. Yet all experiments are conducted exclusively on Walker Walk. The paper_framing.md explicitly acknowledges Cheetah Run as a \"P0 priority\" for addressing the single-environment limitation, and the experiment plan includes 20 experiments across 4 environments (Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole, Hopper). None of the cross-environment experiments appear in the paper. The paper summary provided to reviewers describes extensive cross-environment results (Cheetah mean eval 663, Cartpole 818, Hopper 250, cross-env AUROC 0.58) that do not exist in the actual manuscript. Without cross-environment validation, the paper cannot distinguish whether CIVO is a general vulnerability of dt-conditioning in imagination-based RL or a Walker Walk-specific artifact. The title should be scoped accordingly.\n\n**W2: Extremely small sample sizes undermine quantitative claims.**\nThe NVE diagnostic threshold (1.5x) is evaluated on n=8 seeds total, with n_pos=3 (or n_pos=2 for sustained CIVO). The paper reports AUROC=0.933 and \"7/8 classification,\" but the Clopper-Pearson 95% CI for 87.5% accuracy is [47.3%, 99.7%] -- a confidence interval spanning essentially the entire range. The paper acknowledges this in the appendix but continues to foreground the AUROC metric in the main text as if it were a robust finding. With n=8, the AUROC is not meaningfully distinguishable from chance at conventional significance levels. The 1.5x threshold, while intuitively motivated, is calibrated on the same data used to evaluate it -- there is no held-out validation.\n\n**W3: Baseline normalization introduces fragility.**\nThe NVE metric normalizes by a single baseline seed's v/e trajectory (exp_001, seed 1, 505K steps). Any instability or idiosyncrasy in this single reference trajectory would propagate to all NVE calculations. The paper restricts to a \"35K+ window\" to avoid early-training artifacts, but this window choice is itself a researcher degree of freedom. No sensitivity analysis is provided for the baseline choice (e.g., using seed 2's trajectory instead). Given that 008f seed 2 (009c) shows declining eval to 148 at 105K without any dt-embedding, the baseline variance is non-trivial.\n\n**W4: Confounded experimental comparisons.**\nSeveral key comparisons involve more than one changed variable:\n- 008d (trained, sub ON, H=8, dt=2) vs 008a (trained, sub OFF, H=15, dt=2): differs in both subsampling AND horizon. The paper claims this isolates \"data composition,\" but the 3.2x performance gap between 008a (777.7) and 008c (240.9) demonstrates that horizon effects are substantial.\n- 008d (trained, sub ON, H=8) vs 008f (removed, sub ON, H=8): cleanly isolates dt-embedding, but 008f's reward MAE is 75x worse than 008d. The \"no CIVO\" finding could partly reflect the reward head's inability to predict anything useful, rather than the absence of a specific overestimation mechanism.\n\n**W5: Causal claims exceed the evidence.**\nThe paper uses language like \"causal trigger,\" \"causal chain,\" and \"causal control\" extensively. While the factorial design provides suggestive evidence for necessary conditions, true causal identification requires either (a) randomized intervention with sufficient statistical power, or (b) a formal causal model with testable implications. With 2-3 seeds per condition and no formal hypothesis testing (no p-values, no effect sizes with CIs for the main comparisons), the evidence supports \"strong correlational pattern\" rather than \"causal identification.\" The 1/2 collapse rate for both 008d and 008e_v3 -- identical despite different imagination dt -- is particularly challenging for causal claims about dt>1 imagination as a trigger.\n\n**W6: The \"intervention\" analysis is underpowered.**\nThe uncertainty-gated truncation pilot (mentioned in the task summary) and the full-SGS experiment (exp_018) are described but not analyzed in depth. Exp_018 shows NVE 0.55 and eval 160 with \"gradient starvation\" -- a different failure mode. The paper identifies what goes wrong but provides no viable path forward, weakening the actionable contribution.\n\n**W7: Missing statistical tests for key comparisons.**\nThe paper reports no formal statistical tests comparing conditions. For instance:\n- Is 008d's NVE trajectory significantly different from 008f's? No test provided.\n- Is the 50% collapse rate for trained dt-emb significantly different from the 0% rate for removed dt-emb? With n=2 per group, a Fisher exact test yields p=0.47 -- not significant.\n- The RSSM fidelity analysis reports single-checkpoint values (2.78x reward gap) without confidence intervals or variation across training steps.\n\n**W8: Treatment runs are short (105K) relative to baseline (505K).**\nThe baseline runs for 505K steps and peaks at 970.4; treatments run only to 105K. Some configurations (e.g., 008f seed 1) show continued improvement at 105K (eval 503, still climbing). It is unclear whether the performance gaps observed at 105K would persist, narrow, or reverse at convergence. The paper's 008e_v3 s2 collapse occurs at 95-105K, raising the question of whether 105K is too short to observe late-onset failures in other conditions (e.g., would 008f seed 2 eventually collapse if run to 200K?).\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you attempted to run any configuration on a second environment (e.g., Cheetah Run, which your report.md shows as queued)? Even a single Cheetah baseline + trained dt-emb comparison would substantially strengthen the claims.\n\n2. The NVE threshold is calibrated on the evaluation set. What happens if you use a different baseline seed for normalization? How sensitive is the 7/8 classification to this choice?\n\n3. The gradient decomposition is done at a single checkpoint (step 95K from D061). Does the gradient contribution distribution change over training? Is the 93/7/0 split stable from 25K through 105K?\n\n4. You describe the CIVO mechanism as a 4-stage feedback loop (biased rewards -> optimistic critic -> actor exploitation -> distribution shift). Do you have evidence that Stage 4 (distribution shift reinforcing bias) actually occurs, or does the loop stop at Stage 3?\n\n5. What is the computational cost of each experiment (GPU-hours)? The 105K training budget seems modest -- could longer runs with more seeds be feasible?\n\n6. Regarding the 008f trade-off: the 75x worse reward MAE makes the \"no CIVO\" finding hard to interpret. Have you considered an intermediate ablation -- e.g., a low-dimensional dt-embedding (d=4 or d=8 instead of d=32) -- that might provide some temporal conditioning without triggering full CIVO?\n\n## Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Cross-environment validation** (Cheetah Run, Cartpole, Hopper) -- the most critical gap. The experiment plan includes these (plans 020-052) but none were executed.\n2. **More seeds per condition** -- at minimum 5 seeds for the key 008d and 008f comparison to enable meaningful statistical testing.\n3. **Longer training runs** for treatment conditions (at least 200K, ideally 500K to match baseline).\n4. **Sensitivity to dt-embedding dimension** (d=4, 8, 16 vs the tested d=32).\n5. **Value prediction distribution analysis** (plan_032) -- mentioned in the framing but only reported as summary statistics.\n6. **Formal statistical comparison** between conditions (permutation tests or bootstrap CIs on the NVE gap).\n7. **Ablation on the observation decoder pathway** -- the gradient decomposition identifies this as the dominant channel, but no targeted intervention on this pathway is reported in the paper (exp_018/full-SGS is mentioned but underanalyzed).\n\n## Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n## Justification\n\nThe paper presents a methodologically thoughtful factorial analysis of a real and underexplored problem -- why dt-conditioning fails in imagination-based RL when it succeeds in MPC. The gradient decomposition finding (93% observation decoder, 0% reward head) is genuinely surprising and constitutes a real mechanistic insight. The dual-pathway discovery and honest reporting of partially-falsified hypotheses demonstrate scientific rigor in execution.\n\nHowever, the paper has two fundamental problems that prevent acceptance at a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate. First, all experiments are conducted on a single environment (Walker Walk), making it impossible to assess generality. The title and abstract imply cross-environment analysis that does not exist. Second, the statistical basis is inadequate: n=8 seeds for the diagnostic evaluation, n=2-3 per condition for the main comparisons, no formal significance tests, and confidence intervals so wide as to be uninformative. The causal language throughout the paper is not warranted by the evidence presented.\n\nThe contribution would be substantially stronger with (a) at least one additional environment showing the same CIVO pattern, (b) 5+ seeds per key condition, and (c) formal statistical tests. As submitted, this is a careful preliminary investigation that identifies an interesting phenomenon but lacks the evidence base to support its claims at the level of confidence expected by the venue.","project":"event-gated-adaptive-horizon","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260516T115400","overall_score":"3 - Weak Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":750,"cache_read_input_tokens":77686,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":750,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FXrjvQWSFc6K3k2oYyLyeD","session_id":"2c57719a-f24e-4bf2-bbe7-05d690719237","uuid":"2719e4bf-d439-47f5-9a2a-4ee41acaf0bb"}
